-- title:
--   Geometric function field and curve model of X/ℤ[1/Nqq']
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number, $q,q'$ primes, put $R=\mathbb{Z}[1/(Nqq')]$ (the localisation of $\mathbb{Z}$ away from $Nqq'$), and let $X$ be an integral scheme with a morphism $\pi_X\colon X\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ that is proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$ and whose base change along any morphism $\operatorname{Spec}k\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ with $k$ an algebraically closed field is integral; assume the function field $K(X)$ has characteristic zero and that every element of $K(X)$ algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the image of $\mathbb{Q}$; finally let $\bar s\colon\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ be a morphism over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}$. The conclusion asserts the existence of: instances making $K(X)$ a curve over $\mathbb{Q}$ (degree-zero principal divisors exist, all places have residue field finite over $\mathbb{Q}$, and $\Omega_{K(X)/\mathbb{Q}}$ is free of rank one) and essentially of finite type over $\mathbb{Q}$; a field $\bar F$ with a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra structure, likewise a curve over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and essentially of finite type; a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{toBar}\colon K(X)\to\bar F$; a homomorphism $\mathrm{gal}$ from $\operatorname{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ to the group of pairs (ring automorphism of $\bar F$, ring automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$) compatible with the structure map; a curve model $\mathfrak{M}$ of $\bar F/\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ in the sense of [`AlgebraicCurve.CurveModel`](def/AlgebraicCurve_CurveModel.html#L23) (an integral scheme $\mathfrak{M}.C$, proper and smooth of relative dimension one over $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, with an isomorphism $\mathfrak{M}.\mathrm{ffEquiv}\colon\bar F\cong K(\mathfrak{M}.C)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and a bijection between closed points and places); and an isomorphism $e_{\mathfrak M}\colon \mathfrak{M}.C\to X\times_{\operatorname{Spec}R}\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, such that: $\mathrm{toBar}$ is compatible with $\mathbb{Q}\to\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\to\bar F$; the subfield generated by the images of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and of $\mathrm{toBar}$ is all of $\bar F$; $\mathrm{toBar}$ carries $\mathbb{Q}$-linearly independent finite subsets of $K(X)$ to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-linearly independent families (so $\bar F=\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot K(X)\cong\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}K(X)$); the base component of $\mathrm{gal}(\sigma)$ is $\sigma$ and $\mathrm{gal}(\sigma)$ fixes the image of $\mathrm{toBar}$ pointwise; $e_{\mathfrak M}$ followed by the projection to $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is $\mathfrak{M}.\mathrm{toBase}$; for every nonempty open $U\subseteq X$ with nonempty preimage under $e_{\mathfrak M}$ followed by the projection to $X$, and every section $t$ over $U$, the germ at the generic point of the pulled-back section, read in $\bar F$ through $\mathfrak{M}.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$, equals $\mathrm{toBar}$ of the germ of $t$; for every $\sigma$ and every place $P$ of $\bar F/\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $\mathfrak{M}.C$ attached to $\mathrm{gal}(\sigma)\cdot P$, mapped to $X$, equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\sigma)$ followed by the map to $X$ attached to $P$; and for every place $P$ and $x\in K(X)$, $\mathrm{toBar}(x)$ lies in the valuation subring of $P$ exactly when $x$ lies in the image of the stalk of $X$ at the image point of the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point attached to $P$.
--
--   This is the base-change package for a smooth proper relative curve over $\mathbb{Z}[1/(Nqq')]$: it produces the geometric function field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot K(X)\cong\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}K(X)$ with its semilinear action of $\operatorname{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$, together with a curve model of it identified with the geometric fibre $X_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}$, and records how germs of sections, $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points and valuation subrings on the model correspond to data on $X$. It feeds the Čerednik–Drinfeld side of the argument, being used in the study of fake elliptic curves attached to a quaternionic Shimura curve.
